Dan Anderson, cunningly disguised as dan@mathjunkies.com wrote:

>> What usually kills you?

> 1.  I end  up falling  into /  running into a  large pack  of monsters
>     which hit me all at once and kill me.

Try to always have a clear path back to the upstairs, so you can run away.
Talking about running away, *never*, as long as you're still a beginner,
be burdened or, even worse, stressed/strained/overtaxed. Even slow
monsters might still hit you. As soon as you see something approaching,
drop everything that's encumbering you.

> 2.  I run  out of power  and can't cast  any spells (I usually  play a
>     wizard) and get kiled.

Don't play a wizard. Try a valkyrie to start with. They have many more HP
and are therefore more appropriate for beginners. If you insist on playing
a wizard, use your cat: let it walk out in front of you, so it can soften
up your opponents.

> 3.  Something bad happens in the game -- i.e. I get my armor stolen by
>     a nymph,

Nymphs should *always* be asleep. If they attack you, you did something to
wake them up, like kicking in doors. If at all possible, open doors with
the o command. Use a key or lock pick if a door is locked. If you don't
have one, see if you can take a detour to avoid having to kick down the
door.

>  or I get  cursed and can't  do anything while  a monster
>     beats me  to death,

I'm not sure what you mean by "I get cursed". There aren't any monsters
that can curse you and monsters that curse things in your inventory only
appear much later in the game, around level 20 or so.

> or I am  lucky enough to use  / wear something
>     that is cursed.

Only wear of put on things that you *know* not to be cursed. As long as
you don't put them on, they're harmless. To find out whether they're
blessed/uncursed/cursed, drop them on an altar. If you don't have an altar
handy, drop them on the floor and look if your cat will walk on them. If
it repeatedly refuses to step on them, and won't even follow you in a
corridor if that corridor is "blocked" by the item, it is almost certainly
cursed. If you see your pet stepping on the item, without the message
"Xxxx moves only reluctantly", the item is guaranteed not cursed.

Waering something cursed is never unlucky, always uncareful. The only
exception to this are helmets: some of them curse themselves when you put
them on.

> 4.  I do something dumb (like break open a wand of invisibility to see
>     what it does, and die.

Don't apply wands if you don't know what they are, and even then, be
careful if you're low level. To try out a wand, engrave with them on the
floor. In many cases, this will give you a message that might make it
clear what the wand is.

